---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Stamp from "../components/Stamp.astro";
---
<Base
  title="lantev — small decision heads on Earth embeddings"
  description="Lantev is a family of small, calibrated decision heads on frozen Earth embeddings, with a text bridge. In development; no endpoint exists yet."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 pt-12">
    <p class="stamp-text text-ink-soft">models</p>
    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
      <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] leading-none text-ink-deep">Lantev</h1>
      <Stamp label="in development" />
    </div>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-[1.15rem]">
      A family of small, calibrated decision heads on frozen Earth embeddings — with a text
      bridge, so a class label is a phrase you type rather than an index you memorise.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      The substrate isn't mine: frozen embeddings from Tessera and AlphaEarth. What's being
      built here is the thin part on top — the decision head, the bridge, the calibration,
      and the right to say <em>I don't know</em>. That's the work: labels and an eval suite
      for Southeast Asian land-use vocabulary, before any model.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-12">
      <div class="plate-frame">
        <img
          src="/plates/sumbing.webp"
          width="1400"
          height="944"
          alt="Lithograph of Gunung Sumbing on Java: a conical volcano above jungle and plantations"
          class="block h-auto w-full"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="stamp-text mt-3 text-ink-soft">
        gunung sumbing, lithograph after junghuhn, 1853–54 — the model is supposed to read this without being told what it is
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">Status</h2>
      <p class="mt-4">
        No endpoint, and none will exist until the eval suite does. Before any model ships,
        the hand-checked label-to-class examples ship first — frozen and versioned, so you
        can hold the demo to the same yardstick as the benchmark.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        And if the small head can't beat a plain logistic-regression baseline on a
        spatially held-out test, this page will say that instead of showing a demo.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">Known before launch</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          The embeddings are annual composites; floods and crop cycles are averaged away.
          Do not expect sub-annual change.
        </li>
        <li>
          Shipped probabilities are not calibrated to your ground truth until refit on your
          own data. Treat them as a ranking, not a truth.
        </li>
        <li>
          Never compare results across embedding versions without pinning both the model
          and the dataset version.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
